In a Treap (Tree + Heap) with randomized priorities, how are the split(T, k) and merge(L, R) operations implemented to guarantee O(log n) expected time for all dynamic set operations?', 'void split(Node* t, Key k, Node*& l, Node*& r) {
    if (!t) l = r = nullptr;
    else if (k < t->key)
        split(t->left, k, l, t->left), r = t;
    else
        split(t->right, k, t->right, r), l = t;
}', '[{"id": "A", "text": "By maintaining BST order on keys and max-heap order on assigned priorities, split partitions tree T into L (keys <= k) and R (keys > k) by recursive descent along the search path"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By converting all nodes into a doubly linked list and applying quicksort on priorities"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By calculating AVL height balances on both subtrees simultaneously"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By using splay operations to bring the median priority node to the root"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'A Treap maintains dual invariants: BST order on keys and Heap order on uniformly random priorities. The split(T, k) operation cuts T into two treaps L and R along the key boundary in O(tree height) = O(log n) expected time, enabling lock-free and rope-like dynamic array operations.', ARRAY['Treap', 'Randomized Data Structures', 'Boss Challenge', 'Binary Trees']::text[]);
In a Red-Black Tree insertion, when node z is inserted as a right child of a left child (LR case) and z''s uncle is BLACK, which combined structural transformations restore all Red-Black invariants?', '        grandparent (B)
           /
       parent (R)
           \
            z (R)  [Uncle is BLACK]', '[{"id": "A", "text": "Left-rotate parent, right-rotate grandparent, then swap colors of parent and grandparent"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Recolor uncle and parent to black, recolor grandparent to red, without any tree rotations"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Perform a single right rotation at grandparent, then recolor all leaf nodes"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Delete node z and re-insert it as a root node"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'When uncle is black in an LR violation: 1) Left rotation at parent converts it into an LL (Left-Left) shape. 2) Right rotation at grandparent lifts the new node to root position. 3) Swapping original colors (new root becomes Black, former grandparent becomes Red) restores black-height and no-consecutive-red invariants in O(1) rotation time.', ARRAY['Red-Black Tree', 'Tree Rotations', 'Boss Challenge', 'Invariants']::text[]);
WHERE e1.salary > (SELECT AVG(e2.salary) FROM employees e2 WHERE e2.dept_id = e1.dept_id);', '[{"id": "A", "text": "The correlated subquery is re-evaluated for every single candidate row in the outer query, leading to O(N^2) quadratic behavior without query unnesting"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The database crashes due to memory overflow"}, {"id": "C", "text": "The query fails with syntax error"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It locks the entire database table in exclusive mode"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Medium', 100, 45, 'Because the inner subquery references outer alias e1.dept_id, naive execution runs the subquery for every outer row. Modern query optimizers attempt to unnest this into a Hash Join or Window Function (AVG() OVER (PARTITION BY)).', ARRAY['SQL', 'Subqueries', 'Optimization']::text[]);
-- T1 reads a ($100) and b ($100), withdraws $150 from a
-- T2 concurrently reads a ($100) and b ($100), withdraws $150 from b
-- Both commit successfully under Snapshot Isolation!', '[{"id": "A", "text": "A dirty read where uncommitted changes are displayed"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Two concurrent transactions read overlapping data sets, make disjoint modifications based on stale read assumptions, and both commit, violating a global business invariant"}, {"id": "C", "text": "An unrecoverable crash where log records are written out of order"}, {"id": "D", "text": "A deadlock between primary key indices"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'Hard', 200, 60, 'Snapshot Isolation checks for write-write conflicts on the SAME row. In Write Skew, T1 modifies row A and T2 modifies row B. Neither overwrites the other''s written row, yet together they violate a multi-row integrity constraint.', ARRAY['Snapshot Isolation', 'Write Skew', 'Serializability', 'MVCC']::text[]);
In a concurrent schedule of transactions, how is Conflict Serializability formally proven using a Serialization Graph (Precedence Graph)?', 'Schedule S: r1(x) -> w2(x) -> r1(y) -> w3(y) -> c1 -> c2 -> c3', '[{"id": "A", "text": "By drawing directed edges Ti -> Tj for any conflicting operations where Ti executes before Tj; the schedule is conflict serializable if and only if the precedence graph contains NO directed cycles"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By verifying that all transactions have identical commit timestamps"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By counting total read operations and confirming they exceed write operations"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By checking that all transactions modify distinct tables"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'Two operations conflict if they belong to different transactions, access the same data item, and at least one is a write. An edge Ti -> Tj means Ti must precede Tj. If the precedence graph is acyclic, a topological sort gives an equivalent serial schedule.', ARRAY['Conflict Serializability', 'Precedence Graph', 'Transactions', 'Boss Challenge']::text[]);
Under PostgreSQL''s Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI), how does the engine detect and prevent anomalies like Write Skew without using pessimistic row locks?', NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "By tracking SIREAD locks (predicate locks in memory) to construct dangerous structures in the serialization dependency graph, aborting transactions when two consecutive rw-antidependency edges form a cycle"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By converting every serializable transaction into single-threaded execution on the primary CPU core"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By locking the entire database in exclusive mode during every read query"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By rejecting all transactions that modify more than one table simultaneously"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'PostgreSQL SSI (based on Cahill''s thesis) tracks read-write conflicts using non-blocking SIREAD locks. It detects ''dangerous structures'' where a transaction has both an incoming and outgoing rw-antidependency edge, aborting one transaction with a 40001 serialization failure to prevent write skew while avoiding heavyweight pessimistic locking.', ARRAY['SSI', 'Serializable Snapshot Isolation', 'PostgreSQL', 'Boss Challenge']::text[]);
signal(S): S++;', '[{"id": "A", "text": "A counting semaphore integer value can range over an unrestricted domain, representing the count of available identical resource units"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Counting semaphores cannot be used across processes"}, {"id": "C", "text": "A counting semaphore can only be decremented by the kernel root user"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Binary semaphores allow simultaneous access to 10 threads"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Medium', 100, 45, 'A binary semaphore has values {0, 1} for mutual exclusion. A counting semaphore is initialized to N (the number of available shared resources); calling wait() decrements count, and calling signal() increments it.', ARRAY['Semaphores', 'Concurrency', 'Synchronization']::text[]);
turn = j;
while (flag[j] && turn == j) /* busy wait */;
