flag[i] = false;', '[{"id": "A", "text": "flag indicates process interest; turn resolves simultaneous contention by giving priority to the other process, preventing mutual lockout and deadlock"}, {"id": "B", "text": "One variable is for kernel mode, the other is for user mode"}, {"id": "C", "text": "To notify the CPU scheduler of quantum expiration"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To synchronize memory caches across distinct computers"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Medium', 100, 45, 'Using only flag causes deadlock when both set flag=true at the same instant. Using only turn causes strict alternation without respecting progress. Combining flag (desire to enter) and turn (courtesy tie-breaker) satisfies mutual exclusion, progress, and bounded waiting.', ARRAY['Peterson''s Algorithm', 'Mutual Exclusion', 'Synchronization']::text[]);
-- High task H preempts and requests R, blocking on L
-- Medium task M preempts L (since priority M > L), preventing L from finishing and releasing R to H!
-- Result: Task H is delayed indefinitely by Task M!', '[{"id": "A", "text": "By temporarily elevating the priority of the lower-priority lock holder L to match the highest priority waiting task H until L releases the shared resource"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By aborting the high-priority task immediately"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By disabling multi-threading on the host machine"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By assigning equal priority to all tasks in the operating system"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Medium', 100, 45, 'Priority Inheritance dictates that when high-priority task H blocks on a lock held by low-priority task L, L inherits H''s priority level. This prevents medium tasks M from preempting L, allowing L to swiftly finish its critical section and release R.', ARRAY['Priority Inversion', 'Priority Inheritance', 'RTOS']::text[]);
epoll_ctl(epfd, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, fd, &event);
int n = epoll_wait(epfd, events, MAX_EVENTS, -1);', '[{"id": "A", "text": "epoll maintains an internal Red-Black tree of registered file descriptors and an in-kernel Ready List (linked list) populated directly by device driver wake-up callbacks, returning only active events"}, {"id": "B", "text": "epoll creates a separate thread for every socket"}, {"id": "C", "text": "epoll uses UDP instead of TCP"}, {"id": "D", "text": "epoll compresses file descriptors using Huffman coding"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Hard', 200, 60, 'select/poll require copying the full FD set to kernel space on every call and linearly scanning all N descriptors. epoll registers FDs once in a kernel RB-tree; device wake-up callbacks append ready FDs to a double-linked list, allowing epoll_wait to return in O(ready_events).', ARRAY['epoll', 'I/O Multiplexing', 'Linux Kernel', 'Scalability']::text[]);
// Thread 1 suspended here...
// Thread 2 pops A, pops B, deletes B, re-allocates A and pushes A back!
// Thread 1 resumes: CAS(&head, old_head, old_head->next) succeeds, but old_head->next was freed!', '[{"id": "A", "text": "A value changes from A to B and back to A; CAS succeeds assuming nothing changed, but structural pointers or memory referenced by A were invalidated/freed"}, {"id": "B", "text": "A deadlock between threads A and B"}, {"id": "C", "text": "An error in the ASCII character table"}, {"id": "D", "text": "A hardware interrupt occurring during atomic swap"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Hard', 200, 60, 'CAS only checks bit equality: if memory holds address A, it assumes state has not changed. But if node A was popped, freed, and its address re-allocated while another node was deleted, dangling pointers cause corruption. Mitigated by Hazard Pointers or Generation Tags (tagged pointers / double-word CAS).', ARRAY['Lock-Free', 'ABA Problem', 'CAS', 'Concurrency']::text[]);
In modern multi-socket Non-Uniform Memory Access (NUMA) architectures, how does the OS kernel''s NUMA-aware memory allocator and scheduler prevent cross-interconnect memory latency penalties?', NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "By allocating physical frames from the local NUMA memory node attached directly to the socket where the requesting thread is running, and using CPU affinity / automatic NUMA balancing to migrate memory pages if threads migrate across sockets"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By interleaving every byte across all physical memory channels equally"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By disabling secondary CPU sockets during memory-intensive calculations"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By routing all memory requests through PCIe bus lanes"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'In NUMA systems, accessing remote socket memory over QPI/UPI interconnects takes 2-3x longer than local node RAM. The Linux kernel uses zonelists to satisfy allocations from the local NUMA node first. AutoNUMA periodically unmaps pages, catches page faults, and migrates pages to match thread CPU socket affinity.', ARRAY['NUMA', 'Memory Allocation', 'Kernel Schedulers', 'Boss Challenge']::text[]);
In SMP systems with weak memory models (such as ARMv8 or POWER), why is a simple software flag insufficient to communicate data between threads, requiring Acquire-Release memory barriers?', '// Thread 1:
data = 42;                // Write to payload
ready.store(true, ...);   // Flag notification
// Thread 2:
if (ready.load(...)) {
    assert(data == 42);   // Can FAIL without memory barriers!
}', '[{"id": "A", "text": "Out-of-order execution hardware and store buffers can reorder memory writes so Thread 2 observes ready == true before data == 42 is visible in cache; memory_order_release enforces prior writes finish before the store, and memory_order_acquire prevents subsequent reads from moving before the load"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Because integers cannot be shared across multiple threads in C++"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Because Thread 1 and Thread 2 run on different operating systems"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Because CPU caches do not update until system reboot"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'Weak memory architectures allow aggressive CPU and compiler write-reordering. Without explicit memory barriers (or std::memory_order_release / std::memory_order_acquire), the store to ''ready'' can become globally visible before the store to ''data'', causing the assertion in Thread 2 to fail.', ARRAY['Memory Barriers', 'Acquire-Release', 'Weak Memory Models', 'Boss Challenge']::text[]);
64 bytes from 8.8.8.8: icmp_seq=0 ttl=118 time=14.2 ms', '[{"id": "A", "text": "ping"}, {"id": "B", "text": "traceroute"}, {"id": "C", "text": "netstat"}, {"id": "D", "text": "nslookup"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Easy', 50, 30, 'The ping command sends ICMP Type 8 (Echo Request) packets and measures the elapsed duration until receiving an ICMP Type 0 (Echo Reply), reporting round-trip latency and packet drop rates.', ARRAY['ICMP', 'Ping', 'Diagnostics']::text[]);
