In high-volume internet transit, what is Route Flap Damping (RFC 2439) in BGP, and how does it prevent global routing table instability?', NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "It assigns penalty points to prefixes each time their withdrawal/announcement state flaps; when cumulative penalty breaches a suppress threshold, the route is suppressed from forwarding, decaying exponentially over a half-life until reaching a reuse threshold"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It resets all router interfaces whenever an error packet is detected"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It converts all BGP announcements into static IP routes"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It disables peering sessions with any network located in another continent"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'Rapidly flapping routes (up/down oscillation) cause cascade BGP recalculations across hundreds of thousands of worldwide routers. Damping penalizes unstable prefixes (e.g. 1000 penalty per flap). Once suppressed, the route is withheld until the penalty decays below reuse threshold, shielding global CPUs.', ARRAY['BGP Flap Damping', 'Internet Stability', 'Autonomous Systems', 'Boss Challenge']::text[]);
On modern 100 Gbps networks, how does TCP''s Protection Against Wrapped Sequence Numbers (PAWS) mechanism prevent data corruption when the 32-bit sequence number space wraps around within seconds?', '// At 100 Gbps, 2^32 bytes (~4.29 GB) wraps in ~343 milliseconds!
// How does TCP distinguish old delayed segments from new ones?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "By using the TCP Timestamps option (RFC 7323) to attach a monotonically increasing timestamp to each segment, rejecting arriving segments whose timestamp is older than the current connection''s highest verified timestamp"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By expanding the TCP sequence header to 128 bits dynamically"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By closing and reopening the TCP connection every 300 milliseconds"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By limiting maximum bandwidth to 1 Gbps"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'At high speeds, 4.29 billion sequence numbers wrap faster than the Maximum Segment Lifetime (MSL). A delayed packet from a prior iteration would appear valid within the receiver window. PAWS uses 32-bit millisecond timestamps as a logical high-order sequence extension, discarding segments with TSval < TS.Recent.', ARRAY['PAWS', 'TCP Timestamps', 'High-Performance Networking', 'Boss Challenge']::text[]);
