}', '[{"id": "A", "text": "For positive inputs (x > 0), the derivative of ReLU is a constant 1.0, allowing error gradients to propagate through deep layers without saturating or decaying exponentially"}, {"id": "B", "text": "ReLU is a smooth differentiable function everywhere including zero"}, {"id": "C", "text": "ReLU restricts network weights to positive numbers"}, {"id": "D", "text": "ReLU computes faster than additions"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Medium', 100, 45, 'Sigmoid''s maximum derivative is 0.25; chaining multiple layers multiplies numbers < 0.25, causing gradients to vanish to 0 near early layers. ReLU has derivative 1.0 for x > 0, sustaining robust gradient flow across hundreds of layers.', ARRAY['ReLU', 'Vanishing Gradient', 'Activation Functions']::text[]);
L2 penalty: (lambda / 2) * sum(w_i^2)', '[{"id": "A", "text": "L1 regularization produces sparse models by driving coefficients of non-informative features exactly to zero (acting as feature selection); L2 shrinks weights asymptotically towards zero without setting them strictly to zero"}, {"id": "B", "text": "L2 produces sparse models; L1 increases weight sizes"}, {"id": "C", "text": "L1 is only applicable to neural networks; L2 is only for linear models"}, {"id": "D", "text": "L2 regularization does not have a lambda hyperparameter"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Medium', 100, 45, 'The L1 diamond-shaped constraint region has sharp corners on parameter axes; loss contours typically intersect constraint boundaries at axes where w_i = 0 (sparsity). The L2 spherical constraint shrinks weights smoothly without zeroing them.', ARRAY['L1 vs L2', 'Regularization', 'Lasso', 'Ridge', 'Sparsity']::text[]);
y = gamma * x_hat + beta', '[{"id": "A", "text": "It normalizes each layer''s pre-activation inputs across the current mini-batch to zero mean and unit variance, scaling and shifting by learnable parameters gamma and beta"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It normalizes images to 8-bit integers"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It averages weights across all training epochs"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It normalizes loss to range between 0 and 100"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Medium', 100, 45, 'Batch Norm stabilizes internal layer distribution shifts (internal covariate shift), enabling substantially higher learning rates, acting as a mild regularizer, and reducing sensitivity to weight initialization.', ARRAY['Batch Normalization', 'Deep Learning', 'Optimization']::text[]);
v_t = beta2 * v_{t-1} + (1 - beta2) * g_t^2', '[{"id": "A", "text": "It maintains exponentially decaying moving averages of both past gradients (first raw moment / Momentum) and squared gradients (second uncentered moment / RMSprop), with bias correction for early timesteps"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It switches between SGD and L-BFGS dynamically"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It eliminates the need to calculate gradients entirely"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It trains on secondary CPU cores"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Medium', 100, 45, 'Adam (Adaptive Moment Estimation) accelerates through ravines via momentum m_hat while adapting per-parameter learning rates inversely to the root of historical squared gradient variance sqrt(v_hat).', ARRAY['Adam Optimizer', 'Optimization', 'Deep Learning']::text[]);
where head_i = Attention(Q * W_i^Q, K * W_i^K, V * W_i^V)', '[{"id": "A", "text": "The h attention heads (each of dimension d_k = d_model / h) are concatenated along the feature axis into a tensor of size d_model and multiplied by a final projection matrix W^O"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The heads are averaged using element-wise addition"}, {"id": "C", "text": "The head with the maximum attention weight is selected and others are deleted"}, {"id": "D", "text": "The heads are passed into a convolutional filter"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Hard', 200, 60, 'Multi-head attention allows the model to jointly attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions. Concatenating all h heads (dimension d_v) and multiplying by W^O (dimension h*d_v x d_model) projects the output back to dimension d_model.', ARRAY['Multi-Head Attention', 'Transformers', 'Architecture']::text[]);
In modern Large Language Models (LLaMA, Mistral), how does Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE) encode relative token position into query and key projections?', 'R_Theta,m^d = diag( R_{theta_1, m}, R_{theta_2, m}, ..., R_{theta_{d/2}, m} )
where R_{theta_i, m} is a 2D rotation matrix by angle m * theta_i', '[{"id": "A", "text": "By structuring the projection into 2D vector pairs and rotating each pair by angle m * theta_i in the complex plane, ensuring that the inner product <R_m q, R_n k> depends purely on relative distance (m - n)"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By adding fixed sinusoidal values to input token embeddings before the first layer"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By learning a static lookup table of absolute position vectors"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By scrambling token order randomly during inference"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'RoPE (Su et al.) marries absolute and relative positional embeddings. By representing query and key slices as complex numbers and rotating by m*theta, dot products satisfy <R_m q, R_n k> = Re[ (q * e^(i m theta)) * (k * e^(i n theta))^* ] = Re[ q k^* e^(i (m - n) theta) ], decaying naturally with relative distance.', ARRAY['RoPE', 'Transformers', 'LLMs', 'Mistral', 'Boss Challenge']::text[]);
How does FlashAttention (Dao et al.) achieve 2x-4x training speedup and linear memory footprint on modern GPUs without altering exact mathematical attention outputs?', NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "By tiling query, key, and value blocks to compute attention entirely within high-speed GPU on-chip SRAM, maintaining running Softmax normalization statistics (online softmax trick) to avoid writing the N x N attention matrix to High Bandwidth Memory (HBM)"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By pruning 90% of attention weights using low-rank factorization"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By quantizing all matrix weights to 1-bit binary representations"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By replacing matrix multiplication with fast Fourier transforms"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'Boss', 500, 90, 'Standard attention is memory-bandwidth bound (reading/writing the N x N matrix to slow HBM takes O(N^2) memory). FlashAttention uses GPU kernel fusion and online softmax to evaluate blocks in SRAM (20 TB/s), streaming outputs directly and reducing HBM I/O complexity to sub-quadratic.', ARRAY['FlashAttention', 'GPU Architecture', 'SRAM', 'Deep Learning', 'Boss Challenge']::text[]);
